<h2>Where Water Claims Get Underpaid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cutting drywall too low.</strong> Estimates that replace 2 feet of wall when moisture readings show wetting higher up.</li>
<li><strong>Flooring continuity.</strong> Replacing flooring in one room when the same floor runs through several rooms without a break.</li>
<li><strong>Cabinets and countertops.</strong> Treating swollen base cabinets as dryable, or ignoring damage to countertops during removal.</li>
<li><strong>Insulation and subfloor</strong> under pier-and-beam homes, which are easy to leave out of the scope.</li>
<li><strong>Access costs.</strong> Tear-out and repair needed to reach the leak, often covered even when the pipe is not.</li>
<li><strong>Mold sublimits.</strong> Mold that develops after a covered water loss may be subject to a low limit. See our <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage claims</a> page.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you already have a payment that will not cover the work, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> or <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> review may help identify the gaps.</p>

<h2>Water Losses in Rental and Commercial Buildings</h2>
<p>Landlords and business owners face extra complications. A leak in one apartment can damage the unit below, and the building owner’s policy usually covers the structure while each tenant’s renters policy covers their own belongings. Commercial buildings may have sprinkler leakage coverage, equipment breakdown coverage for failed boilers or chillers, and business income coverage if the space has to close during repairs. Leases may assign repair responsibilities for plumbing fixtures and HVAC equipment. Gather the lease, any maintenance logs, and the tenant’s contact information early, and notify every insurer that may be involved.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help With Water Claims</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder. On a water claim, that may include identifying and documenting the source, mapping moisture with meters and thermal imaging, reviewing the mitigation company’s logs, preparing a line-item repair estimate, and addressing exclusions such as seepage or wear. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can also help when the insurer has denied the claim as a long-term leak by gathering plumber reports and other evidence of timing. No outcome is guaranteed. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
